      "name": "Imagine",
      "type": "urn:entity",
      "properties": {
        "release_year": 1972,
        "plot_themes_description": "Examines the tension between celebrity and ordinary domestic life while exploring avant‑garde artistic expression and personal identity through surreal vignettes.",
        "description": "Imagine (1972) is a television-length, music-oriented documentary featuring John Lennon and Yoko Ono (with a credited appearance by Fred Astaire) that pairs songs from the Imagine and Fly albums with an array of surreal visual vignettes. The film intercuts staged, often absurd sketches with candid household and home‑movie footage, producing a dreamlike montage that ranges from playful spoofs to tender domestic scenes. Recurring motifs—chess, boats, fog, and eccentric performance pieces—evoke avant‑garde texts and experimental art practices, while the subject matter probes fame, identity, and intimacy. The tone is intimate and provocative, appealing to viewers interested in music, art, and countercultural expression."
