        "description": "The Rolling Stones: Cocksucker Blues is a 1972 documentary directed by Robert Frank that follows the Rolling Stones on their North American tour. Filmed in both Super‑8 color and black‑and‑white, the movie intercuts concert performances with candid backstage scenes—sex, drug use, groupies and celebrity encounters—presented in a raw, cinéma‑vérité style. With nonlinear montage and inventive sound editing, the film functions as an observational study of fame, excess and alienation on the road; its candid content and controversial nature have limited authorized screenings. Notable appearances include Truman Capote, Dick Cavett and Marshall Chess."
